#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-tmux-ledger — what the tmux server had running, so a server crash costs a restart, not the work.

  cc-tmux-ledger save              write this server's windows to its ledger (tmux.conf's after-new-window hook)
  cc-tmux-ledger watch             tmux.conf starts it at server start: when the last server crashed, starts
                                   cc-recover (which brings the sessions back), then saves every 30 s while this
                                   server lives; one per server. It relaunches nothing itself
  cc-tmux-ledger restore           by hand: relaunch, in a window of the same name, cwd and command, every window
                                   the dead server had running that is not live now. Refused while cc-recover holds
                                   its lock, so the two never start the same window twice
  cc-tmux-ledger list              what the dead server had running that is not live now, and the one command
                                   that brings it back
  cc-tmux-ledger forget            tmux-main.service's ExecStop, before its kill-server: a deliberate stop (`!restart
                                   tmux`, a stop of the unit, a clean reboot) is not a crash, so the next server
                                   starts clean. With no live server, or under systemd with no MAINPID (the unit's
                                   server already died), it does nothing: that was a crash
  cc-tmux-ledger selfcheck         a real scratch tmux server, killed with SIGSEGV twice

WHY. The tmux 3.6 server segfaulted on 2026-10-01 and again on 10-03 (kernel: "tmux: server segfault at 10 …
error 4"). Every seat, orch and --go loop lived in that one server, so all of them died at once, tmux-main
recreated only the box window, and nothing recorded what had been live: each one was found and restarted by hand.

THE LEDGER is ~/.cc/tmux-ledger/<socket>.json (mode 700, and not under ~/.cc/state, where a member workspace's
handle names a directory), one per tmux socket, and only the default socket keeps one:
a test fixture's scratch server that reads ~/.tmux.conf writes nothing here. CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET names another
socket (`tmux -L`) and CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR another directory, for the selfcheck and for a hand run. Each window is
recorded once (grouped sessions share their windows) with its session, name, start directory (the cwd its command
was started in, not wherever the pane has cd'd since), command and state:
  running   the pane is still doing its work
  ended     the pane's process is a bare interactive shell: the work finished and only the "shell stays" prompt is
            left (cc's workers and seats end `…; exec bash`), even if someone has since typed a command into it.
            Never relaunched, because a finished --go worker relaunched would run a whole new iteration.
  shell     the window was opened with no command. Never relaunched.
The command is the pane's own `sh -c` argument read from /proc when it is still there, and otherwise tmux's
#{pane_start_command} decoded — never eval'd, because tmux leaves backticks and > unescaped in it, and an unquoted
one (several arguments) only when it is all plain characters. A command neither way recovers is listed and not
relaunched.

A DEAD SERVER'S LEDGER becomes <socket>.lost.json the first time anything runs against a server other than the one the
ledger names, when that one is no longer a tmux server. A server is its pid and the boot it ran in (the kernel's
boot_id), in the ledger and in forget's marker alike, because pids recur across boots: a new server that reuses the
old pid after a reboot is still a new server. That holds whichever runs first after a crash — the
new server's first save or its watcher — so the new server never overwrites the list of what the old one lost.
Two crashes before a restore merge into one lost list. `restore` removes each entry it relaunched or found live.
Each entry keeps the time its ledger was last saved, which is within 30 s of the crash, and an entry over a day old
is dropped. An entry or a file of the wrong shape is skipped, never a crash.

AFTER A CRASH, cc-recover is the one thing that relaunches sessions: it resumes each seat and orch on its own
conversation, refuses a record it cannot trust, skips what is live, and takes a lock (~/.cc/recover/.lock) so a
second trigger does nothing. Archiving a crash marks the lost list `recover: due`, and the watcher, finding it so,
starts cc-recover-restore.service (CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER names another command, for the selfcheck) and marks it
`sent`, under the ledger's lock, so one crash starts it once. A second crash within 15 minutes of the last start
(the box may be crashing on what it restored) backs off: it stays `due` with an `after` time, announced once, and
the watcher starts cc-recover when the wait is over, a wait that doubles with each such crash in a row.

Every name, session and cwd handed back to tmux has its # doubled: tmux expands #(…) and #{…} in -n, -s and -c,
so a window named `#(cmd)` would otherwise run cmd. The command itself and the -t targets are not expanded.
tmux prints a window name or start directory raw, newlines included, so the one list-panes a save makes ends each
record in a random token and is split on that, and a pane whose record is not one line of the expected fields is
read again with its own `display -p`, then skipped and logged: a directory named "x\n<fields>" must not add a
window to the ledger that restore would then run.
A dead pane (remain-on-exit) is left out. A name or path that is not UTF-8 is kept byte for byte, and a save that
fails anyway is logged while the watcher carries on: a dead watcher would be crash protection off without a word.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not stop the crash (a tmux upgrade is the owner's), and a window a hand `restore`
relaunches starts a new conversation unless its own command resumes one (cc's `__run` and `__runmember` do):
`cc-recover restore` is the way back for a seat or an orch.
"""
import fcntl
import json
import os
import secrets
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

INTERVAL = float(os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_INTERVAL", "30"))
GAP = float(os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_GAP", "2"))   # seconds between relaunches, so thirty seats do not start at once
GUARD = 15 * 60                                           # a crash inside this of the last cc-recover start waits before starting it again
BACKOFF_MAX = 4 * 60 * 60                                 # …a wait that doubles with each such crash in a row, up to this
EXPIRE = 24 * 60 * 60                                     # an entry older than this leaves the lost list
SHELLS = {"bash", "sh", "dash", "zsh", "fish", "-bash", "-sh", "-zsh"}
SEP = "\x1f"


def socket_name():
    s = os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET")
    if s:
        return s
    t = os.environ.get("TMUX", "")
    return os.path.basename(t.split(",")[0]) if t else "default"


def allowed():
    # Only the box's own server keeps a ledger unless a socket is named outright: a fixture server is never ours.
    return bool(os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET")) or socket_name() == "default"


def statedir():
    d = os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/tmux-ledger")
    os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    return d


def path(kind):
    return os.path.join(statedir(), f"{socket_name()}.{kind}")


def tmux(*args, check=False):
    cmd = ["tmux"] + (["-L", os.environ["CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET"]] if os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET") else []) + list(args)
    # tmux prints names and paths as raw bytes: surrogateescape keeps a byte that is not UTF-8 (0xff) and gives it
    # back to tmux unchanged, where strict decoding would raise and stop the watcher
    r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape")
    if check and r.returncode:
        raise RuntimeError(f"tmux {' '.join(args[:2])}: {r.stderr.strip()}")
    return r


def server_pid():
    r = tmux("list-sessions", "-F", "#{pid}")
    if r.returncode or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    return int(r.stdout.split()[0])


def boot_id():
    try:
        with open("/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id") as f:
            return f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


BOOT = boot_id()


def is_tmux_server(pid):
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/comm") as f:
            return f.read().strip() == "tmux: server"
    except OSError:
        return False


def alive(pid, boot):
    """pid is a live tmux server of this boot. A pid alone names no server: pids recur across boots."""
    return bool(pid) and boot == BOOT and is_tmux_server(pid)


def log(msg):
    with open(path("log"), "a", errors="backslashreplace") as f:
        f.write(time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z ") + msg + "\n")


def read_json(p):
    """A ledger or lost list as a dict, or None: unreadable, not JSON, or JSON of another shape."""
    try:
        with open(p) as f:
            obj = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    return obj if isinstance(obj, dict) else None


def windows(obj):
    """The entries of a ledger that have the shape restore needs; anything else is skipped, not a crash."""
    ws = obj.get("windows") if isinstance(obj, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(ws, list):
        return []
    return [w for w in ws if isinstance(w, dict)
            and all(isinstance(w.get(k), str) for k in ("session", "name", "state"))
            and isinstance(w.get("cwd") or "", str) and isinstance(w.get("cmd") or "", str)
            and isinstance(w.get("start") or "", str)]


def pid_of(obj, key):
    v = obj.get(key) if isinstance(obj, dict) else None
    return v if isinstance(v, int) and not isinstance(v, bool) else 0


def boot_of(obj):
    v = obj.get("boot") if isinstance(obj, dict) else None
    return v if isinstance(v, str) else ""


def when(w):
    v = w.get("saved")
    return v if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool) else 0


def lit(s):
    """A string tmux would format-expand (-n, -s, -c), made to mean itself."""
    return s.replace("#", "##")


def write_json(p, obj):
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=os.path.dirname(p), prefix=".tmp-")
    text = json.dumps(obj, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)
    try:
        text.encode()
    except UnicodeEncodeError:   # a byte that is not UTF-8 in a name or path: written as \udcff, read back the same
        text = json.dumps(obj, indent=1)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
        f.write(text)
    os.replace(tmp, p)


class Lock:
    def __init__(self, kind="lock", block=True):
        self.f = open(path(kind), "a")
        self.block = block

    def __enter__(self):
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_EX | (0 if self.block else fcntl.LOCK_NB))
        except BlockingIOError:
            self.f.close()
            return None
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        self.f.close()


PLAIN = set("ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789 _./=:,+@%-")


def decode(s):
    """tmux's #{pane_start_command} back to the string it ran, or None when that cannot be done for certain.
    tmux wraps an argument with a special character in "…" and escapes \\ " $ inside (or in '…' with no escapes);
    a VIS escape (\\n, \\342) or a second quoted argument means it is not one string we can give back exactly.
    Unquoted, it is one word or several arguments joined by spaces, and tmux leaves ` > < ( * ? ! unquoted there:
    restore hands the command to sh -c, so `tail -f /dev/null x\\`id\\`` would run id and `x>~/.bashrc` would empty
    the file. Such a command is given back only when every character is a plain one (PLAIN); else it is listed."""
    if len(s) >= 2 and s[0] == s[-1] == "'":
        inner = s[1:-1]
        return None if "'" in inner else inner
    if len(s) >= 2 and s[0] == s[-1] == '"':
        out, i, inner = [], 0, s[1:-1]
        while i < len(inner):
            c = inner[i]
            if c == "\\":
                if i + 1 < len(inner) and inner[i + 1] in '\\"$':
                    out.append(inner[i + 1]); i += 2; continue
                return None
            if c == '"':
                return None
            out.append(c); i += 1
        return "".join(out)
    return s if s and all(c in PLAIN for c in s) else None


def argv(pid):
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb") as f:
            return [a.decode(errors="surrogateescape") for a in f.read().split(b"\0")[:-1]]
    except OSError:
        return []


FIELDS = ["#{pane_id}", "#{window_id}", "#{session_name}", "#{session_group}", "#{window_name}", "#{pane_index}",
          "#{pane_pid}", "#{pane_start_path}", "#{pane_start_command}", "#{pane_dead}"]
_SKIPPED = set()   # panes already logged as unreadable, so the watcher logs each once, not every 30 s


def panes():
    """Each pane as its fields, or None when tmux cannot be asked. One list-panes reads every pane, so a save is one
    tmux client and not one per pane. tmux prints a window name or start directory raw, newlines and all, so a line
    is not a record: a directory named "x\n<fields>" would add a window of its own. Each record therefore ends in a
    token made at random for this read, which no name or path can hold, and the answer is split on that; a record
    starts with its pane id (%N, which holds no separator). A record that is not exactly the expected fields on one
    line is read again on its own with display -p, and one still not right is skipped and logged."""
    end = SEP + secrets.token_hex(16) + "\n"
    r = tmux("list-panes", "-a", "-F", SEP.join(FIELDS) + end[:-1])
    if r.returncode:
        return None
    recs = r.stdout.split(end)
    if recs[-1]:   # the answer stops mid-record (the server died as it answered): no list rather than half of one
        return None
    out, bad, ids = [], [], set()
    for rec in recs[:-1]:
        f = rec.split(SEP)
        pid = f[0]
        if not (pid[:1] == "%" and pid[1:].isdigit()):
            bad.append(repr(rec[:20])); continue
        if pid in ids:   # a grouped session lists its panes again
            continue
        ids.add(pid)
        if len(f) != len(FIELDS) or "\n" in rec:
            d = tmux("display", "-p", "-t", pid, "-F", SEP.join(FIELDS))
            if d.returncode:   # the server died mid-read: half a list must not replace the last whole one
                return None
            line = d.stdout[:-1] if d.stdout.endswith("\n") else None
            f = line.split(SEP) if line is not None and "\n" not in line else []
            if line is not None and not line.strip(SEP):
                continue   # the pane closed since the listing: tmux answers with empty fields
            if len(f) != len(FIELDS) or f[0] != pid:
                bad.append(pid); continue
        if f[-1] != "1":   # a dead pane (remain-on-exit) runs nothing, and its pid may be someone else's by now
            out.append(f[1:-1])
    new = [p for p in bad if p not in _SKIPPED]
    if new:
        _SKIPPED.update(new)
        log(f"skipped pane(s) {' '.join(new)}: tmux's answer was not one line of {len(FIELDS)} fields "
            "(a newline or separator in a window name or start directory)")
    return out


def snapshot():
    ps = panes()
    if ps is None:
        return None
    seen, wins = set(), []
    for wid, sess, group, name, idx, pid, cwd, start in ps:
        if wid in seen:   # a grouped session lists the same window again; its first pane is the window's
            continue
        seen.add(wid)
        av = argv(int(pid)) if pid.isdigit() else []
        if not start:
            state, cmd = "shell", None
        elif len(av) == 1 and os.path.basename(av[0]) in SHELLS:   # what it runs now is someone's typing, not the work
            state, cmd = "ended", decode(start)
        else:
            state = "running"
            cmd = av[2] if len(av) >= 3 and av[1] == "-c" and os.path.basename(av[0]) in SHELLS else decode(start)
        wins.append({"session": group or sess, "name": name, "cwd": cwd, "state": state, "cmd": cmd, "start": start})
    return wins


def forgotten():
    """The server forget marked, as (pid, boot), or (0, "") when there is no marker or it cannot be read."""
    try:
        with open(path("forget")) as f:
            pid, boot = (f.read().split() + ["", ""])[:2]
        return int(pid), boot
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return 0, ""


def archive(cur):
    """Under the lock: a ledger a dead server wrote becomes (or joins) the lost list, unless it was stopped on purpose."""
    gone = forgotten()
    if gone[0] and gone != (cur, BOOT) and not alive(*gone):
        os.remove(path("forget"))   # that server is dead now; its marker has done its work
    led = read_json(path("json"))
    if led is None:
        if os.path.exists(path("json")):   # not a ledger at all: set aside, so the saves go on
            os.remove(path("json"))
            log("removed a ledger file that was not a ledger")
        return
    spid, sboot = pid_of(led, "server_pid"), boot_of(led)
    if (spid, sboot) == (cur, BOOT) or alive(spid, sboot):
        return
    if spid and (spid, sboot) == gone:
        os.remove(path("json"))
        log(f"server {spid} was stopped on purpose: nothing to restore")
        return
    lost = read_json(path("lost.json"))
    keep = {(w["session"], w["name"]): w for w in windows(lost)}
    for w in windows(led):
        if w["state"] == "running":
            keep[(w["session"], w["name"])] = dict(w, saved=when(w) or when(led))
    lost = {"server_pid": spid, "boot": sboot, "saved": when(led), "windows": list(keep.values()), "recover": "due"}
    write_json(path("lost.json"), lost)
    os.remove(path("json"))
    log(f"server {spid or '?'} is gone: {len(lost['windows'])} running window(s) on the lost list")


def save():
    if not allowed():
        return 0
    cur = server_pid()
    if cur is None:
        return 0
    with Lock():
        archive(cur)
        if forgotten() == (cur, BOOT):   # being stopped on purpose: a save now would make the stop look like a crash
            return 0
        wins = snapshot()
        if wins is None:
            return 0
        write_json(path("json"), {"server_pid": cur, "boot": BOOT, "saved": int(time.time()), "windows": wins})
    return 0


def live_names():
    r = tmux("list-windows", "-a", "-F", "#{session_group}" + SEP + "#{session_name}" + SEP + "#{window_name}")
    out = set()
    for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
        g, s, n = (line.split(SEP) + ["", "", ""])[:3]
        out.add((g or s, n))
    return out


def pending():
    lost = read_json(path("lost.json")) or {}
    live, now = live_names(), time.time()
    return lost, [w for w in windows(lost) if (w["session"], w["name"]) not in live and now - when(w) < EXPIRE]


def forget():
    if not allowed():
        return 0
    cur = server_pid()
    if cur is None:   # the server is already dead, so this is a crash's ExecStop: what it lost stays to be restored
        return 0
    mp = os.environ.get("MAINPID", "")   # systemd's: the server the unit is stopping, if it is still alive
    if mp and mp != str(cur):   # a server someone else started after the unit's one died is not being stopped
        return 0
    if os.environ.get("INVOCATION_ID") and not mp:   # under systemd with no MAINPID: the unit's server already died
        print("cc-tmux-ledger: forget did nothing: under systemd with no MAINPID, the unit's own server already died",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    with Lock():
        with open(path("forget"), "w") as f:
            f.write(f"{cur} {BOOT}")
        for k in ("json", "lost.json"):
            if os.path.exists(path(k)):
                os.remove(path(k))
    log(f"server {cur} is being stopped on purpose: the next server starts clean")
    return 0


def describe(ws):
    return ", ".join(w["name"] for w in ws)


def notify(msg):
    # Only the box's own server tells anyone: a selfcheck or a hand run on a named socket never reaches Slack.
    if os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET") or not shutil.which("cc-notify"):
        return
    subprocess.run(["cc-notify", "-t", "tmux server restarted", msg], capture_output=True)


def recover_lock():
    """cc-recover's own lock, ~/.cc/recover/.lock, taken without waiting: an open fd while held, None while another
    holder (a cc-recover restore) has it. Whoever relaunches sessions holds it, so two never start the same one."""
    d = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cc", "recover")
    os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    fd = os.open(os.path.join(d, ".lock"), os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_CLOEXEC, 0o600)
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except BlockingIOError:
        os.close(fd)
        return None
    return fd


def recover_cmd():
    c = os.environ.get("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER")
    return shlex.split(c) if c else ["systemctl", "--user", "start", "--no-block", "cc-recover-restore.service"]


def recover():
    """The watcher's one step after a crash: start cc-recover, once per crash, and never relaunch anything here.
    A crash inside GUARD of the last start may be the box crashing on what it restored, so it backs off: cc-recover
    starts after a wait that doubles with each such crash in a row (GUARD, 2x, 4x … up to BACKOFF_MAX), and the
    first crash of the run posts one line (owner 2026-10-20: back off and keep trying, never leave the box down).
    The stamp's mtime is the last start and its text how many quick crashes in a row led to it."""
    if not allowed():
        return 0
    with Lock():
        lost = read_json(path("lost.json"))
        if not lost or lost.get("recover") != "due":
            return 0
        stamp, now = path("recovered"), time.time()
        if "after" not in lost and os.path.exists(stamp) and now - os.path.getmtime(stamp) < GUARD:
            try:
                quick = int(open(stamp).read().strip() or 0) + 1
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                quick = 1
            wait = min(GUARD * 2 ** (quick - 1), BACKOFF_MAX)
            write_json(path("lost.json"), dict(lost, after=now + wait, quick=quick))
            if quick == 1:
                msg = (f"The tmux server died again within {GUARD // 60} minutes of the last cc-recover start, so "
                       f"cc-recover waits {wait // 60} minutes before it brings the sessions back, and longer if it "
                       "keeps crashing. `cc-recover restore` brings them back now.")
                log(msg); notify(msg); print(msg)
            else:
                log(f"the tmux server died again soon after a recover: cc-recover waits {wait // 60} minutes")
            return 0
        if now < float(lost.get("after") or 0):
            return 0
        with open(stamp, "w") as f:
            f.write(str(int(lost.get("quick") or 0)))
        write_json(path("lost.json"), dict(lost, recover="sent"))
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(recover_cmd(), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        rc, err = r.returncode, (r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:200]
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        rc, err = 127, str(e)
    if rc == 0:
        log("the tmux server died: cc-recover started to bring the sessions back")
    else:
        msg = (f"The tmux server died and cc-recover could not be started (rc={rc} {err}). "
               "`cc-recover restore` brings the sessions back.")
        log(msg); notify(msg)
    return 0


def restore():
    if not allowed():
        return 0
    cur = server_pid()
    if cur is None:
        print("cc-tmux-ledger: no tmux server is running", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    held = recover_lock()
    if held is None:
        print("cc-tmux-ledger: cc-recover is bringing the sessions back right now; run this again once it is done",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    try:
        return relaunch(cur)
    finally:
        os.close(held)


def relaunch(cur):
    with Lock():
        archive(cur)
        lost, todo = pending()
        if not todo:
            if os.path.exists(path("lost.json")):
                os.remove(path("lost.json"))
            return 0
        done, skipped = [], []
        for w in todo:
            if not w.get("cmd"):
                skipped.append(w); continue
            if (w["session"], w["name"]) in live_names():   # something else (cc-recover, a seat's own revival) got there first
                done.append(w); continue
            cwd = w["cwd"] if os.path.isdir(w["cwd"] or "") else os.path.expanduser("~")
            # recurring-defect-ok: pause-hold-missing — this relaunches the window's own command, and a worker's is cc-loop, which asks `cc-pause is <repo>` before it starts anything
            # -t is matched literally, but -n, -s and -c are format-expanded: lit() keeps #(…) in a name from running
            has = tmux("has-session", "-t", "=" + w["session"]).returncode == 0
            if has:
                r = tmux("new-window", "-d", "-t", "=" + w["session"] + ":", "-n", lit(w["name"]), "-c", lit(cwd), w["cmd"])
            else:
                r = tmux("new-session", "-d", "-s", lit(w["session"]), "-n", lit(w["name"]), "-c", lit(cwd), w["cmd"])
            (done if r.returncode == 0 else skipped).append(w)
            if GAP:
                time.sleep(GAP)
        left = [w for w in todo if w not in done]   # one already live counts as back
        if left:
            write_json(path("lost.json"), dict(lost, windows=left))
        else:
            os.remove(path("lost.json"))
    msg = f"Brought back {len(done)} window(s) the tmux server lost: {describe(done) or 'none'}."
    if skipped:
        msg += f" Not brought back, because their command could not be recovered or would not start: {describe(skipped)}."
    log(msg); print(msg)
    save()
    return 0


def listing():
    if not allowed():
        return 0
    _, todo = pending()
    for w in todo:
        print(f"{w['session']}:{w['name']}\t{w.get('cwd') or ''}\t{w.get('cmd') or '(command not recoverable) ' + (w.get('start') or '')}")
    if todo:
        print("cc-tmux-ledger restore   brings these back")
    return 0


def guarded(fn, **kw):
    """The watcher's one call: a failure is logged and the watcher carries on, because a dead watcher is crash
    protection switched off without a word."""
    try:
        fn(**kw)
    except Exception as e:
        log(f"{fn.__name__} failed, the watcher carries on: {e!r}")


def watch():
    if not allowed():
        return 0
    for _ in range(60):   # tmux.conf runs before the server's first session exists
        cur = server_pid()
        if cur:
            break
        time.sleep(0.5)
    else:
        return 0
    with Lock(f"watch-{cur}", block=False) as held:   # a second `source-file` must not start a second watcher
        if not held:
            return 0
        guarded(save)   # moves a dead server's ledger to the lost list before anything can overwrite it
        guarded(recover)   # a crash just found: cc-recover brings the sessions back, this relaunches nothing
        while server_pid() == cur:
            guarded(save)
            guarded(recover)   # a crash that is backing off starts cc-recover once its wait is over
            time.sleep(INTERVAL)
    try:
        os.remove(path(f"watch-{cur}"))
    except OSError:
        pass
    return 0


SELFCHECK = r'''
set -u
B="$1"; n=0; f=0
k(){ n=$((n+1)); if [ "$1" = 0 ]; then echo "  ok   $2"; else echo "  FAIL $2"; f=$((f+1)); fi; }
T=$(mktemp -d); SOCK="cctl-$$-$RANDOM"
export HOME="$T/home" CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET="$SOCK" CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR="$T/state" CC_TMUX_LEDGER_GAP=0 CC_TMUX_LEDGER_INTERVAL=1
export CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER="$T/recover"   # stands in for cc-recover-restore.service: counts its starts
mkdir -p "$HOME" "$T/state" "$T/w/a dir" "$T/w/d#(echo x)y"
printf '#!/bin/sh\necho started >> %s/recover.calls\n' "$T" > "$T/recover"; chmod +x "$T/recover"
calls(){ if [ -f "$T/recover.calls" ]; then wc -l < "$T/recover.calls"; else echo 0; fi; }
X(){ tmux -L "$SOCK" "$@"; }
trap 'X kill-server 2>/dev/null; rm -rf "$T"' EXIT
L="CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET=$SOCK CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR=$T/state CC_TMUX_LEDGER_GAP=0 CC_TMUX_LEDGER_INTERVAL=1 CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER=$T/recover $B"
cat > "$T/tmux.conf" <<C
set-hook -g after-new-window 'run-shell -b "$L save >/dev/null 2>&1"'
run-shell -b "$L watch >/dev/null 2>&1"
C
boot(){ X -f "$T/tmux.conf" new-session -d -s main -n box -c "$T/w" 'sleep 600'; }   # what tmux-main.service does
upto(){ local i; for i in $(seq 60); do eval "$1" && return 0; sleep 0.25; done; return 1; }
has(){ X list-windows -t main -F '#W' 2>/dev/null | grep -qxF "$1"; }
HS='o#(echo pwn)'; HW='w#{pane_id}#(echo pwn)'; HD="$T/w/d#(echo x)y"   # tmux would run or expand these if not escaped
hashed(){ X list-windows -t "=$HS" -F '#W' 2>/dev/null | grep -qxF "$HW"; }
inledger(){ python3 -c "import json,sys; sys.exit(0 if sys.argv[1] in [x['name'] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['windows']] else 1)" "$1" 2>/dev/null; }
saved(){ python3 -c "import json; print(json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['saved'])" 2>/dev/null || echo 0; }
fresh(){ local s0; s0=$(saved); upto "[ \"\$(saved)\" -gt $s0 ]"; }   # one more save by the watcher
boot
# work: counts its own starts. odd/one@x: a command tmux quotes with $, backticks, quotes and a dash — its output
# must be byte-identical after the relaunch. fin: finished, its shell stays (cc's `…; exec bash`). bare: no command.
X new-window -d -t main: -n work -c "$T/w" "cd /; echo started >> $T/work.starts; sleep 600"
X new-window -d -t main: -n 'odd/one@x' -c "$T/w/a dir" "printf '%s|' \"\$PWD\" '\$HOME' \"a \\\"b\\\"\" '[shell stays — ok]' >> $T/odd.out; echo >> $T/odd.out; sleep 600"
X new-window -d -t main: -n fin -c "$T/w" "echo done; exec bash"
X new-window -d -t main: -n bare -c "$T/w"
X new-session -d -s main-1 -t main   # a grouped client session: its windows are main's, recorded once
# A session of its own (the boot makes only main, so the relaunch has to create it), whose session, window name and
# cwd hold #(…) and #{…}: made here with ## so tmux stores them as written.
X new-session -d -s 'o##(echo pwn)' -n 'w##{pane_id}##(echo pwn)' -c "$T/w/d##(echo x)y" "pwd >> $T/hash.out; sleep 600"
upto '[ -s $T/odd.out ] && [ -s $T/hash.out ] && python3 -c "import json,sys; w=json.load(open(\"$T/state/$SOCK.json\"))[\"windows\"]; sys.exit(0 if len(w)==6 and [x[\"state\"] for x in w if x[\"name\"]==\"fin\"]==[\"ended\"] else 1)" 2>/dev/null'
k $? "the ledger holds each window once, grouped sessions or not, and a finished one is 'ended'"
# Someone types a command into a finished worker's leftover shell: it is still finished, not the worker again.
X send-keys -t main:fin 'sleep 600' Enter
upto 'pgrep -P "$(X display -p -t main:fin "#{pane_pid}")" >/dev/null'; k $? "a command typed into the finished window is running"
fresh
st=$(python3 -c "import json; print(' '.join(x['name']+'='+x['state'] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['windows']))")
k "$([ "$st" = "box=running work=running odd/one@x=running fin=ended bare=shell $HW=running" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "states, fin still 'ended' with that command under it: $st"
wc=$(python3 -c "import json; print([x['cwd'] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['windows'] if x['name']=='work'][0])")
k "$([ "$wc" = "$T/w" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a window's cwd is the one its command started in, not where it has cd'd since: $wc"
spid=$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1); first=$(head -1 "$T/odd.out")
kill -SEGV "$spid"; upto '! kill -0 $spid 2>/dev/null'; k $? "SIGSEGV kills the scratch server, as the real one died"
boot
upto '[ "$(calls)" = 1 ]'; k $? "after the crash the new server's watcher starts cc-recover"
fresh; fresh
k "$(! has work && ! has 'odd/one@x' && [ "$(wc -l < $T/work.starts)" = 1 ] && [ "$(calls)" = 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…once, and relaunches nothing itself: cc-recover is the one relauncher"
out=$("$B" list); k "$(grep -q '^main:work' <<<"$out" && grep -qF 'main:odd/one@x' <<<"$out" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "list names the windows the dead server had running"
"$B" restore >/dev/null
upto 'has work && has odd/one@x'; k $? "a hand restore brings back the windows that were running"
upto '[ "$(wc -l < $T/work.starts)" = 2 ]'; k $? "…by running their command again (work started twice)"
upto '[ "$(wc -l < $T/odd.out)" = 2 ]'; second=$(sed -n 2p "$T/odd.out")
k "$([ -n "$first" ] && [ "$first" = "$second" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…the same command, byte for byte, in the same cwd: '$second'"
k "$(has fin || has bare && echo 1 || echo 0)" "a finished window and a bare shell are not relaunched"
upto hashed; k $? "a session that no longer exists is made again, its session and window named as written: '$HS' '$HW'"
upto '[ "$(wc -l < $T/hash.out)" = 2 ]'
k "$([ "$(sort -u "$T/hash.out")" = "$HD" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…in its own cwd, the #(…) in the path left alone: $(tail -1 "$T/hash.out")"
k "$([ "$(X list-windows -t main -F '#W' | grep -cx box)" = 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "the window the new server made itself is not doubled"
k "$([ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "the lost list is cleared once everything on it is back"
# A second crash inside 15 minutes: the box may be crashing on what it restored, so cc-recover waits before starting again.
upto 'python3 -c "import json,sys; sys.exit(0 if \"work\" in [x[\"name\"] for x in json.load(open(\"$T/state/$SOCK.json\"))[\"windows\"]] else 1)" 2>/dev/null'
spid=$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1); kill -SEGV "$spid"; upto '! kill -0 $spid 2>/dev/null'
boot; upto '[ -s $T/state/$SOCK.log ] && grep -q "died again" $T/state/$SOCK.log'
k "$([ $? = 0 ] && [ "$(calls)" = 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a second crash within 15 minutes is logged and does not start cc-recover yet"
k "$(has work; [ $? = 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…work is not running"
mkdir -p "$HOME/.cc/recover"
out=$( { flock -n 9 && "$B" restore; } 9>"$HOME/.cc/recover/.lock" 2>&1 ); rc=$?
k "$([ $rc = 1 ] && grep -q 'cc-recover is bringing' <<<"$out" && ! has work && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a hand restore is refused while cc-recover holds its lock, and starts nothing: $out"
out=$("$B" list); k "$(grep -q '^main:work' <<<"$out" && grep -q '^cc-tmux-ledger restore' <<<"$out" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "list names what was lost and the one command"
"$B" restore >/dev/null; k "$(has work && has 'odd/one@x' && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a hand restore brings them back"
k "$([ -z "$("$B" list)" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…and list is then empty"
upto '[ "$(wc -l < $T/hash.out)" = 3 ]'
k "$(hashed && [ "$(sort -u "$T/hash.out")" = "$HD" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a second relaunch leaves the #s alone too"
# A deliberate stop: tmux-main's ExecStop runs forget, then kill-server. The next server must start clean.
upto 'inledger work'; spid=$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1)
MAINPID=1 "$B" forget
k "$([ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.forget" ] && [ -e "$T/state/$SOCK.json" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "forget leaves alone a live server that is not the one systemd is stopping (MAINPID)"
out=$(env -u MAINPID INVOCATION_ID=selfcheck "$B" forget 2>&1)
k "$([ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.forget" ] && [ -e "$T/state/$SOCK.json" ] && grep -q 'no MAINPID' <<<"$out" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…nor under systemd with no MAINPID, when the unit's own server already died, and it says so: $out"
MAINPID=$spid "$B" forget; X kill-server; upto '! kill -0 $spid 2>/dev/null'
k "$([ -e "$T/state/$SOCK.forget" ] && [ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.json" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "forget marks the live server and drops its ledger"
boot; upto '[ -e $T/state/$SOCK.json ]' && fresh
k "$(! has work && [ "$(calls)" = 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a deliberate stop (forget, then kill-server) starts no cc-recover and brings nothing back"
k "$([ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json" ] && [ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.forget" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…and leaves no lost list and no marker behind"
# On a crash systemd runs ExecStop too, after the server is gone: forget must then do nothing.
X new-window -d -t main: -n work2 -c "$T/w" "sleep 600"
upto 'inledger work2'; spid=$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1); kill -SEGV "$spid"; upto '! kill -0 $spid 2>/dev/null'
"$B" forget; rm -f "${T:?}/state/$SOCK.recovered"
boot; upto '[ "$(calls)" = 2 ]'
k "$([ $? = 0 ] && grep -q '^main:work2' <<<"$("$B" list)" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "forget after the server already died (a crash's ExecStop) changes nothing: the crash starts cc-recover and work2 is listed"
# Age: an entry over a day old is dropped. Entries of the wrong shape sit beside them and are skipped.
python3 - "$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json" "$T/w" <<'P'
import json, sys, time
now = int(time.time())
w = lambda n, age: {"session": "main", "name": n, "cwd": sys.argv[2], "state": "running", "cmd": "sleep 600", "start": "sleep 600", "saved": now - age}
json.dump({"server_pid": 1, "saved": now, "windows": [w("fresh1", 60), w("old1", 7200), w("gone1", 90000),
           1, "x", {"session": 3, "name": "bad1"}, {"name": "bad2"}, dict(w("bad3", 60), cmd=["sleep"])]}, open(sys.argv[1], "w"))
P
out=$("$B" list); k "$(grep -q '^main:old1' <<<"$out" && grep -q '^main:fresh1' <<<"$out" && ! grep -q gone1 <<<"$out" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "an entry over a day old is dropped from the list; an hour-old one stays"
out=$("$B" restore 2>&1); rc=$?
k "$([ $rc = 0 ] && has fresh1 && has old1 && ! has gone1 && ! has bad3 && ! grep -q Traceback <<<"$out" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a hand restore brings back both, and skips the wrong-shaped entries without a crash: $out"
lostlist(){ python3 - "$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json" "$T/w" "$@" <<'P'
import json, sys, time
now = int(time.time())
ws = [{"session": s, "name": n, "cwd": sys.argv[2], "state": "running", "cmd": "sleep 600", "start": "sleep 600",
       "saved": now - int(a)} for s, n, a in (x.split(":") for x in sys.argv[3:])]
json.dump({"server_pid": 1, "boot": "", "saved": now, "windows": ws}, open(sys.argv[1], "w"))
P
}
lostnames(){ python3 -c "import json; print(' '.join(x['name'] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json'))['windows']))" 2>/dev/null; }
# A relaunch tmux refuses (an empty session name) is reported and stays on the list; the one beside it comes back.
lostlist :nostart1:60 main:fresh2:60
out=$("$B" restore 2>&1); rc=$?
k "$([ $rc = 0 ] && has fresh2 && grep -q 'would not start: nostart1' <<<"$out" && [ "$(lostnames)" = nostart1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a relaunch that fails is named and kept on the lost list: $out"
# A ledger and a lost list of the wrong shape must not stop the watcher's saves.
echo '[1, 2]' > "$T/state/$SOCK.json"; echo '"x"' > "$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json"
upto 'python3 -c "import json,sys; sys.exit(0 if json.load(open(\"$T/state/$SOCK.json\")).get(\"server_pid\") else 1)" 2>/dev/null'
k $? "a ledger of the wrong shape is set aside and the watcher keeps saving"
out=$("$B" restore 2>&1); rc=$?
k "$([ $rc = 0 ] && ! grep -q Traceback <<<"$out" && [ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.lost.json" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…and a lost list of the wrong shape is cleared, not a crash: rc=$rc $out"
# Pids recur across boots: a ledger or a forget marker naming the live server's pid from another boot names a dead server.
cur=$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1); rm -f "${T:?}/state/$SOCK.lost.json"
flock "$T/state/$SOCK.lock" python3 - "$T/state/$SOCK.json" "$cur" "$T/w" <<'P'   # under the lock, so a watcher save in flight cannot overwrite it
import json, os, sys, time
now = int(time.time())
w = {"session": "main", "name": "prevboot1", "cwd": sys.argv[3], "state": "running", "cmd": "sleep 600", "start": "sleep 600"}
json.dump({"server_pid": int(sys.argv[2]), "boot": "another-boot", "saved": now, "windows": [w]}, open(sys.argv[1] + ".t", "w"))
os.replace(sys.argv[1] + ".t", sys.argv[1])
P
"$B" save; out=$("$B" list)
k "$(grep -q '^main:prevboot1' <<<"$out" && python3 -c "import json,sys; sys.exit(0 if json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['boot'] == open('/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id').read().strip() else 1)" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a ledger naming the live pid from another boot is a dead server's: its windows go on the lost list"
rm -f "${T:?}/state/$SOCK.lost.json"; printf '%s another-boot\n' "$cur" > "$T/state/$SOCK.forget"; s0=$(saved); sleep 1.1; "$B" save
k "$([ ! -e "$T/state/$SOCK.forget" ] && [ "$(saved)" -gt "$s0" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a forget marker naming the live pid from another boot is cleared, and the saves go on"
# The server check is the exact process name, so another process named "tmux…" (a client's is "tmux") is not a server.
d=$(python3 - "$B" "$spid" "$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1)" <<'P'
import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, subprocess, sys, time
ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("l", sys.argv[1]); m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader("l", ld)); ld.exec_module(m)
p = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, "-c", "import ctypes, time; ctypes.CDLL(None).prctl(15, b'tmux: client', 0, 0, 0); time.sleep(30)"])
for _ in range(50):
    if open(f"/proc/{p.pid}/comm").read().strip() == "tmux: client":
        break
    time.sleep(0.1)
r = [m.is_tmux_server(int(sys.argv[3])), open(f"/proc/{p.pid}/comm").read().strip() == "tmux: client",
     m.is_tmux_server(p.pid), m.is_tmux_server(int(sys.argv[2]))]
p.kill()
print(r == [True, True, False, False])
P
)
k "$([ "$d" = True ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "only a process named exactly 'tmux: server' counts as a live server"
# A fixture server reading ~/.tmux.conf on another socket keeps no ledger: only the default socket or a named one.
before=$(ls "$T/state" | sort); out=$(env -u CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET TMUX="/tmp/tmux-0/fixture,1,0" "$B" save; echo rc=$?)
k "$([ "$out" = rc=0 ] && [ "$(ls "$T/state" | sort)" = "$before" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a scratch server on an unnamed socket writes nothing"
# tmux prints a start directory and a window name raw, newlines included: one made of a newline and forged fields
# must not become a ledger entry that restore would run.
U=$'\x1f'; EV="$T/w/nl"$'\n'"@99${U}main${U}${U}forged${U}0${U}1${U}$T${U}touch $T/PWNED"
mkdir -p "$EV"
X new-window -d -t main: -n victim -c "$EV" 'sleep 600'
X new-window -d -t main: -n "nm"$'\n'"%0${U}@98${U}main${U}${U}forged2${U}0${U}1${U}$T${U}touch $T/PWNED2" 'sleep 600'
# The line after the newline is exactly the right fields and starts with the window's own pane id, so a reader that
# trusted "a pane id and the right field count" would take it as a window @97 named forged3. (rename-window escapes
# a newline, new-window -n does not, so the id is the next one tmux hands out.)
X new-window -d -t main: -n idprobe 'sleep 600'; P=$(X display -p -t main:idprobe '#{pane_id}'); X kill-window -t main:idprobe
P="%$(( ${P#%} + 1 ))"
X new-window -d -t main: -n "s"$'\n'"$P${U}@97${U}main${U}${U}forged3" 'sleep 600'
k "$([ "$(X list-panes -a -F '#{pane_id}' | grep -cxF "$P")" -ge 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "the window whose name forges a record of its own pane id $P exists"
"$B" save
fg=$(python3 -c "import json; print(' '.join(x['name'] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['windows'] if 'forged' in x['name'] or 'PWNED' in (x.get('cmd') or '') or x['name'] == 'victim'))")
k "$([ -z "$fg" ] && inledger fresh2 && grep -q 'skipped pane' "$T/state/$SOCK.log" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a window whose start directory or name holds a newline and forged fields is skipped and logged, not forged into the ledger: '$fg'"
# A window name and start directory holding a byte that is not UTF-8 (0xff) must not kill save or the watcher,
# and both are recorded so that tmux gets the same bytes back.
FF="$T/w/f"$'\xff'"f"; mkdir -p "$FF"
X new-window -d -t main: -n "ff"$'\xff' -c "$FF" 'sleep 600'
out=$("$B" save 2>&1); rc=$?
ff=$(python3 -c "import json,sys; print(sum(x['name'] == 'ff\udcff' and x['cwd'] == sys.argv[1] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['windows']))" "$FF" 2>&1)
k "$([ $rc = 0 ] && [ -z "$out" ] && [ "$ff" = 1 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a window name and start directory holding byte 0xff are saved, not a crash: rc=$rc '$ff' $out"
fresh; k $? "…and the watcher goes on saving with that window open"
# A command of several arguments comes back from tmux unquoted, with ` and > left as they are: restore must not hand
# it to sh -c. A cc launcher's command, one string that tmux quotes, still comes back.
printf '#!/bin/sh\ntouch %s/PWNED3\n' "$T" > "$T/pw"; chmod +x "$T/pw"; echo keep > "$T/clobber"
printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "$*" >> %s/ccl.starts\nexec sleep 600\n' "$T" > "$T/fakecc"; chmod +x "$T/fakecc"
X new-window -d -t main: -n evil1 -c "$T/w" env "X=x\`$T/pw\`" sleep 600
X new-window -d -t main: -n evil2 -c "$T/w" env "X=x>$T/clobber" sleep 600
X new-window -d -t main: -n ccl -c "$T/w" "$(printf 'CC_CLAUDE=%q %q __run %q %q %q' claude "$T/fakecc" demo row '')"
upto 'inledger evil1 && inledger evil2 && inledger ccl && [ -s $T/ccl.starts ]'
ev=$(python3 -c "import json; print(' '.join(x['name'] for x in json.load(open('$T/state/$SOCK.json'))['windows'] if x['name'] in ('evil1', 'evil2') and x['state'] == 'running' and x['cmd'] is None and x['start'].startswith('env X=x') and not set(x['start']) & set(chr(34) + chr(39) + chr(92))))")
k "$([ "$ev" = "evil1 evil2" ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "a running command of several arguments, tmux leaving its \` or > unquoted, is saved with no command: '$ev'"
c0=$(calls); spid=$(X list-sessions -F '#{pid}' | head -1); kill -SEGV "$spid"; upto '! kill -0 $spid 2>/dev/null'
rm -f "${T:?}/state/$SOCK.recovered"; boot
upto '[ "$(calls)" = $((c0 + 1)) ]' && "$B" restore >/dev/null
upto 'has ccl && [ "$(wc -l < $T/ccl.starts)" = 2 ]'; k $? "after a crash a hand restore relaunches a cc launcher's command (one quoted string): $(tail -1 "$T/ccl.starts")"
upto 'grep -q "could not be recovered.*evil1" $T/state/$SOCK.log'
k "$(! has evil1 && ! has evil2 && [ ! -e "$T/PWNED3" ] && [ "$(cat "$T/clobber")" = keep ] && grep -q 'could not be recovered.*evil2' "$T/state/$SOCK.log" && echo 0 || echo 1)" "…and the two are listed and logged, not run: no backtick command ran and no file was truncated"
# panes() and the watcher, with tmux answering as it does mid-crash: stubbed, so each answer is exactly the one meant.
d=$(python3 - "$B" "$T/imp" <<'P'
import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, json, os, sys, types
os.environ.update(CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR=sys.argv[2], CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET="imp")
ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("l", sys.argv[1]); m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader("l", ld)); ld.exec_module(m)
S, ans, lp, calls, cut = m.SEP, {}, [], [], [0]
row = lambda p, w, dead="0": S.join([p, w, "main", "", "n" + w, "0", "1", "/", "sleep 9", dead])
def tmux(*a, check=False):
    calls.append(a[0])
    if a[0] == "list-sessions":
        rc, out = 0, "4242\n"
    elif a[0] == "list-panes":   # each record ends in the token panes() put after the fields in its format
        out = "".join(x + a[3][len(S.join(m.FIELDS)):] + "\n" for x in lp)
        rc, out = 0, out[:len(out) - cut[0]]
    else:
        rc, out = ans[a[3]]
    return types.SimpleNamespace(returncode=rc, stdout=out, stderr="")
m.tmux = tmux
# (a) the server dies partway: while a malformed record is read again, or mid-answer. No list, and the last whole
# ledger stays as it was
lp, ans = [row("%1", "@1"), S.join(["%2", "@2", "main"])], {"%2": (1, "")}
led = json.dumps({"server_pid": 4242, "boot": m.BOOT, "saved": 1, "windows": []})
with open(m.path("json"), "w") as f:
    f.write(led)
a = m.panes() is None and m.save() == 0 and open(m.path("json")).read() == led
lp, cut[0] = [row("%1", "@1"), row("%4", "@4")], 5
a = a and m.panes() is None and m.save() == 0 and open(m.path("json")).read() == led
# (b) one list-panes for the lot; only a malformed record is read again. A pane that closed before that read (empty
# fields, rc 0), a dead one and a grouped session's repeat are left out; the others are kept
lp, cut[0] = [row("%1", "@1"), S.join(["%2", "@2"]), row("%3", "@3", "1"), row("%4", "@4"), row("%1", "@1")], 0
ans = {"%2": (0, S * (len(m.FIELDS) - 1) + "\n")}
calls.clear(); ps = m.panes()
b = ([p[0] for p in ps] == ["@1", "@4"] and all(len(p) == len(m.FIELDS) - 2 for p in ps) and not m._SKIPPED
     and calls == ["list-panes", "display"])
# (c) a save that raises is logged and the watcher carries on until its server is gone
pids = iter([4242, 4242, 4242])
m.server_pid = lambda: next(pids, None)
def save():
    raise UnicodeDecodeError("utf-8", b"\xff", 0, 1, "stub")
m.save, m.INTERVAL = save, 0
c = m.watch() == 0 and open(m.path("log")).read().count("save failed, the watcher carries on") == 3
print(*(int(not x) for x in (a, b, c)))
P
)
read -r da db dc <<<"$d"
k "${da:-1}" "a tmux answer cut off, or a re-read that fails, gives no pane list, and save leaves the last ledger as it was: $d"
k "${db:-1}" "a save is one list-panes, a malformed record alone is read again, and a closed or dead pane is left out"
k "${dc:-1}" "a save that raises is logged and the watcher carries on"
# Two watchers finding the same crash at once (a second server start, a hand run): cc-recover is started once.
d=$(python3 - "$B" "$T/race" <<'P'
import json, os, subprocess, sys
os.makedirs(sys.argv[2])
env = dict(os.environ, CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR=sys.argv[2], CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET="race",
           CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER=f"sh -c 'sleep 0.3; echo x >> {sys.argv[2]}/calls'")
with open(f"{sys.argv[2]}/race.lost.json", "w") as f:
    json.dump({"server_pid": 1, "boot": "", "saved": 1, "windows": [], "recover": "due"}, f)
code = ("import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, sys; ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader('l', sys.argv[1]); "
        "m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader('l', ld)); ld.exec_module(m); sys.exit(m.recover())")
ps = [subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, "-c", code, sys.argv[1]], env=env) for _ in range(2)]
rcs = [p.wait() for p in ps]
calls = open(f"{sys.argv[2]}/calls").read().count("x") if os.path.exists(f"{sys.argv[2]}/calls") else 0
state = json.load(open(f"{sys.argv[2]}/race.lost.json")).get("recover")
print(int(not (rcs == [0, 0] and calls == 1 and state == "sent")), rcs, calls, state)
P
)
k "${d%% *}" "two watchers that find the same crash at once start cc-recover exactly once: $d"
# Unset, the command is the systemd unit (named only, never run here); a start that fails is logged and stays `sent`.
d=$(python3 - "$B" "$T/fail" <<'P'
import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, json, os, sys
os.makedirs(sys.argv[2])
os.environ.update(CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR=sys.argv[2], CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET="fail")
os.environ.pop("CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER", None)
ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("l", sys.argv[1]); m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader("l", ld)); ld.exec_module(m)
a = m.recover_cmd() == ["systemctl", "--user", "start", "--no-block", "cc-recover-restore.service"]
os.environ["CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER"] = "false"
with open(m.path("lost.json"), "w") as f:
    json.dump({"server_pid": 1, "boot": "", "saved": 1, "windows": [], "recover": "due"}, f)
b = (m.recover() == 0 and "cc-recover could not be started (rc=1" in open(m.path("log")).read()
     and json.load(open(m.path("lost.json")))["recover"] == "sent")
print(int(not a), int(not b))
P
)
read -r da db <<<"$d"
k "${da:-1}" "with no override the watcher starts cc-recover-restore.service through systemctl --user, without waiting"
# A crash soon after a start backs off, then starts cc-recover: it is never held for good.
d=$(python3 - "$B" "$T/back" <<'P'
import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, json, os, sys, time
os.makedirs(sys.argv[2])
os.environ.update(CC_TMUX_LEDGER_DIR=sys.argv[2], CC_TMUX_LEDGER_SOCKET="back",
                  CC_TMUX_LEDGER_RECOVER=f"sh -c 'echo x >> {sys.argv[2]}/calls'")
ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("l", sys.argv[1]); m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader("l", ld)); ld.exec_module(m)
calls = lambda: open(f"{sys.argv[2]}/calls").read().count("x") if os.path.exists(f"{sys.argv[2]}/calls") else 0
lost = lambda: json.load(open(m.path("lost.json")))
def crash():
    with open(m.path("lost.json"), "w") as f:
        json.dump({"server_pid": 1, "boot": "", "saved": 1, "windows": [], "recover": "due"}, f)
out, sys.stdout = sys.stdout, open(os.devnull, "w")       # recover() prints its one line too
open(m.path("recovered"), "w").close()                      # a start a moment ago
crash(); m.recover(); m.recover()
a = calls() == 0 and lost()["recover"] == "due" and lost()["quick"] == 1 and open(m.path("log")).read().count("waits 15 minutes") == 1
d = dict(lost(), after=time.time() - 1); json.dump(d, open(m.path("lost.json"), "w")); m.recover()
b = calls() == 1 and lost()["recover"] == "sent" and open(m.path("recovered")).read() == "1"
crash(); m.recover()                                       # …and it crashes again at once: the wait doubles
c = calls() == 1 and lost()["quick"] == 2 and 29 * 60 < lost()["after"] - time.time() <= 30 * 60
sys.stdout = out
print(int(not a), int(not b), int(not c))
P
)
read -r ba bb bc <<<"$d"
k "${ba:-1}" "a crash inside 15 minutes of a start waits, says so once, and keeps the crash due"
k "${bb:-1}" "…once the wait is over the watcher starts cc-recover"
k "${bc:-1}" "…and a crash straight after that waits twice as long"
k "${db:-1}" "a cc-recover that cannot be started is logged, and the crash is not started again"
# decode never guesses: tmux's quoting is reversed exactly or the command is listed instead.
d=$(python3 - "$B" <<'P'
import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, sys
ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("l", sys.argv[1]); m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader("l", ld)); ld.exec_module(m)
cases = [('"a \\"b\\" \\$x `y` \\\\z"', 'a "b" $x `y` \\z'), ("plain", "plain"), ("'a \"b'", 'a "b'),
         ('"a\\nb"', None), ('"a" "b"', None), ('"\\342\\200\\224"', None),
         ("tail -f /dev/null x`id`", None), ("tail -f /dev/null x>/home/u/.bashrc", None), ("a x<y", None),
         ("a (y)", None), ("a x*", None), ("a x?", None), ("a x!y", None), ("a x\ty", None), ("a ~x", None), ("", None),
         ("tail -f /dev/null x", "tail -f /dev/null x"), ("/home/u/bin/cc-rc", "/home/u/bin/cc-rc"),
         ('"CC_CLAUDE=claude /home/u/bin/cc __run demo row \'\'"', "CC_CLAUDE=claude /home/u/bin/cc __run demo row ''")]
print(sum(m.decode(s) != want for s, want in cases))
P
)
k "$([ "$d" = 0 ] && echo 0 || echo 1)" "decode reverses tmux's quoting and refuses what it cannot reverse exactly"
echo "cc-tmux-ledger selfcheck: $((n - f)) passed, $f failed"
[ "$f" -eq 0 ]
'''


def selfcheck():
    for k in ("TMUX", "CC_SLACK_ALIAS"):
        os.environ.pop(k, None)
    return subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", SELFCHECK, "selfcheck", os.path.abspath(__file__)]).returncode


def main(a):
    cmd = a[0] if a else ""
    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):   # a window name that is not UTF-8 must not make a print raise
        s.reconfigure(errors="backslashreplace")
    if cmd == "save":
        return save()
    if cmd == "watch":
        signal.signal(signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIG_IGN)
        return watch()
    if cmd == "restore":
        return restore()
    if cmd == "list":
        return listing()
    if cmd == "forget":
        return forget()
    if cmd == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()
    print("usage: cc-tmux-ledger save | watch | restore | list | forget | selfcheck", file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
